{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": [
      "dom",
      "dom.iterable",
      "ES2022"
    ],
    "allowJs": false,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    /* Which @types packages are loaded as AMBIENT globals.
       Without this TypeScript loads every directory under node_modules/@types,
       which is not the four this app declares: it is those four plus whatever
       the toolchain drags in transitively. Today that means chai, estree, json5,
       minimist, fs-extra, normalize-package-data and slice-ansi, none of which
       this application imports and all of which it would then depend on being
       installed perfectly.
       That dependency is not theoretical. Four of those directories were once
       left empty by an interrupted install, and the app's typecheck failed with
       four TS2688 errors naming Node CLI tooling it has never used.
       Only globals belong here. Anything reached through an import, which is
       how @types/leaflet and @types/geojson are used, resolves through
       moduleResolution and needs no entry. */
    "types": [
      "node",
      "react",
      "react-dom"
    ],
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ],
    "paths": {
      "@/*": [
        "./src/*"
      ]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "**/*.ts",
    "**/*.tsx",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    /* ONE GLOB, NOT THIRTY LINES. Every parallel build directory this repo
       has ever used, .next-verify, .next-admin, .next-qa5 and the rest, was
       listed by hand, and each new one appended two more lines and another
       commit. They are all the same shape, so match the shape.

       AND NEXT WILL KEEP ADDING THE LITERALS BACK ANYWAY, which is worth
       knowing before somebody thinks the globs are broken. `next build` and
       `next dev` append their own `<distDir>/types` entry to this array
       unless they find that exact STRING already in it; a glob that covers
       the path is not a match as far as that check is concerned. With several
       builds each on their own NEXT_DIST_DIR, this file grew thirty dead
       lines in an evening and showed up as a modified shared file in
       everybody's `git status`.

       So: the two globs below cover every one of them, any `.next-*` literal
       that reappears here is redundant, and you may delete it on sight
       without checking what it was for. */
    ".next-*/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-*/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-a2b/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-a2b/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-b1b/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-b1b/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-b2/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-b2/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-c1/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-c1/dev/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-nav2/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next-nav2/dev/types/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    ".next-*"
  ]
}
